Ladybird keeps one extension side panel per window and resolves the
page it shows from the active tab’s override, else the extension’s
global options, closing the panel where it’s disabled; open() takes a
window id, a tab id, or both when they agree.

Chrome’s SidePanelService keeps PanelOptions per extension with a
default entry and per-tab entries (GetOptions(), SetOptions()), and its
OpenSidePanelForWindow() refuses with “No active side panel for
windowId: N” when the global options have no path or are disabled, as
OpenSidePanelForTab() does for a tab; SidePanelOpenFunction refuses
first with “At least one of `tabId` and `windowId` must be provided”
and “The specified tab does not belong to the specified window.”.
Chrome keeps a global panel’s document across tabs and shows a
tab-specific one on its tab alone.

Firefox’s SidebarController shows one sidebar per window, shared by
the extensions and the built-in sidebars; ext-sidebarAction.js keeps a
panel per tab and per window (getTargetFromDetails(), setPanel()),
refuses tabId and windowId together, and drops a tab’s panel once the
tab navigates somewhere else.

WebKit’s WebExtensionContext keeps a WebExtensionSidebar per window
and per tab (getSidebarWithIdentifiers()), refuses both identifiers at
once, and its sidebarOpen() refuses a disabled sidebar (“the sidebar is
not enabled”) and one with no panel (“no sidebar panel is set”).
